Z||000007||theogre||06-09-2001||08:40 PM||||All synthetics are compatible with traditional oil. They have to be. <P>They are most often put in systems that previously contained crude based product. You can never get all the old oil drained.<P>you have to be able to use crude to add a quart when low and you can't get the synthetic.<P>I'm not sure I'd mix brands of synthetics unless I had to. There are a number of ways to make them. You likely would not fry a motor but you can be 100% sure that all the additives and things will play nice. I avoid mixing crude or antifreeze brands for allot of the same reason. Again, probly would not fry a motor, but could reduce effectiveness of detergents and corrosion inhibiters.<P>Castrol apparently does use crude as some part of it's Syntec product. This is a really huge bone right now between the different oil companies. It could potentially be headed to the courts as a consumer fraud type of thing. I've been trying to follow this in the auto service trade press but there's not allot of info to be had.||||reg||1
